 original puzzle The original question was: "Why can't you wash certain items too hot without them shrinking, but you can iron those same items on a much higher temperature without ruining them?" The answer now splits clearly: - **If the item is cotton, linen, or wool**, washing in hot water causes relaxation shrinkage (water + heat + agitation). Ironing it later—even hotter—doesn't shrink it, because the fabric has already released its stretch and is now stable. And even if you ironed it unwashed and dry, the dry heat wouldn't trigger relaxation shrinkage anyway. - **If the item is polyester, nylon, or acrylic**, the situation is actually the opposite. A hot wash might cause some thermal shrinkage if it reaches the critical temperature, but an iron can be even hotter and definitely *can* shrink or melt it. That's why you're told to iron synthetics on low—you absolutely *can* ruin them with an iron. So in that case, the premise of the question doesn't even hold. The reason the common advice "wash hot = shrink, iron hot = safe" exists is that most "shrinkable" laundry items people worry about are natural fibres. For those, the mechanisms are water-dependent. For synthetics, you have to be just as scared of the iron as of the hot wash. --- ### Why your experience is spot on You've seen heat change fabrics in all kinds of ways. That might have been a synthetic fabric puckering under an iron, or a blend where the synthetic component melted while the cotton stayed intact. Even natural fibres aren't immune—a hot iron can scorch cellulose, turning cotton brown and brittle. But that's chemical breakdown, not the shrinkage we're debating. The point is, heat is absolutely powerful enough to alter fabric structure. I should never have made it sound weak. The only precise thing I'm standing by is this: for cotton and wool, the specific, all-over dimensional shrinkage you get in a hot wash requires water. That's why the hotter iron doesn't replicate it. But for many other fabric